SETVBUF(3)                     P32W Unix                      SETVBUF(3)

NAME
       setvbuf, setbuf - choose a stream's buffering

SYNOPSIS
       #include <stdio.h>

       int setvbuf(FILE *f, char *buf, int mode, size_t size);
       void setbuf(FILE *f, char *buf);

DESCRIPTION
       setvbuf sets how the stream f buffers its output:

       _IOFBF  fully: written out when the buffer (BUFSIZ bytes)
               is full, or on fflush or fclose
       _IOLBF  by lines: also at each newline
       _IONBF  not at all: each character is written at once

       Output already buffered is written out first. stdout is
       line-buffered on a terminal and fully buffered otherwise,
       until a program chooses; stderr is unbuffered.

       The stream keeps its own buffer: buf and size are accepted
       but not used (C allows this).

       setbuf(f, NULL) is setvbuf(f, NULL, _IONBF, 0); otherwise
       setbuf is setvbuf(f, buf, _IOFBF, BUFSIZ).

RETURN VALUE
       setvbuf returns 0, or nonzero if mode is not one of the
       three (or a buffer could not be had).

EXAMPLES
       setvbuf(stdout, NULL, _IOLBF, 0);   /* a line at a time,
                                              even into a pipe */

SEE ALSO
       stdio(3), fopen(3), putc(3)
